course: dictionary
lesson: 5581
title: Big words with a long history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have been around for thousands of years. Leviathan comes from the Bible, where it is a huge sea monster. Today it can also mean anything very large and powerful, like a huge ship or a giant company.
explain: The words around a word give you clues about its meaning. «Livyatan was a sperm whale relative with teeth up to 36 centimetres that hunted other whales at the same time as megalodon; it is named after the Leviathan of old stories.» Here Leviathan means the old sea monster from stories.
explain: In news, you might read «a corporate leviathan», which means a huge, powerful company. Ask yourself what the word would have to mean for the sentence to make sense, then check your meaning by putting it into the sentence.
fact: Leviathan is a noun. One is a leviathan, and more than one is leviathans.
fact: «Leviathan» means a huge sea monster from the Bible, or anything very large and powerful.
fact: Leviathan with a capital L is also the title of a famous 1651 book by Thomas Hobbes about the power of government.
fact: Phrases you may meet are «a leviathan of the deep» and «a leviathan of a ship».
fact: When you see a word from long ago, the context in the sentence will often tell you which meaning the writer means.
exercise: In the Bible, what is a leviathan? | *A huge sea monster | A small wooden boat | A long, quiet river | A kind of bird
exercise: «The company was a corporate leviathan, with offices in every country.» What does leviathan mean here? | A type of song | A sleepy animal | *Something very large and powerful | Something very small
exercise: How do you write more than one leviathan? | leviathani | leviathen | leviathanes | *leviathans
test: «Livyatan was a sperm whale relative... it is named after the Leviathan of old stories.» What are the old stories about? | A friendly dog | A kind of cloud | A small fish in a pond | *A giant sea monster
test: In 1651, Thomas Hobbes wrote a famous book with what title? | The Iliad | *Leviathan | Treasure Island | Beowulf
exam: A leviathan of a ship is best described as | *A huge, powerful ship | A ship made of paper | A ship that never moves | A tiny rowing boat
